# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
# titan-life (Miya's Note). Script from miya 2026-11-06 (dated 2026-10-17); fact-checked per guideline 12-1 (see script-check.md).
SCENES = [
 ('S01', 'Rain Without Water', 'ill', 'p01a', 'What if it rained on another world, but not a single drop was water? Imagine floating down through an orange haze toward a moon of Saturn, with clouds of liquid methane above you and rivers carving valleys below.'),
 ('S02', 'Rain Without Water', 'ill', 'p01b', 'The temperature outside is around minus one hundred and eighty degrees Celsius, cold enough to turn water ice into rock. This is Titan, and NASA takes seriously the question of whether something could be alive there.'),
 ('S03', 'Rain Without Water', 'ill', 'p01c', 'How could life exist in a place that cold, with no liquid water on the surface to drink? The answer might change what we think life even is.'),
 ('S04', 'Follow the Water', 'ill', 'p02a', 'Keep that question in mind, because it is the thread that runs through everything that follows. Much of the search for life is really a search for water. Follow the water is the old rule of thumb.'),
 ('S05', 'Follow the Water', 'ill', 'p02b', 'Titan asks what happens when we find a world with plenty of liquid, but a liquid that is nothing like the one in our oceans. Is that a dead end, or the doorway to a whole new kind of biology?'),
 ('S06', 'An Orange Marble', 'ill', 'p03a', "If you stripped away Saturn's rings and stared solely at Titan, you might mistake it for an eerie, orange marble. It is the only moon in our entire solar system wrapped in a thick, dense atmosphere."),
 ('S07', 'An Orange Marble', 'ill', 'p03b', "If you stood on its frozen surface, the air pressing against your shoulders would be about one and a half times the pressure at Earth's sea level. Nitrogen dominates its golden haze, just like our own air. Yet stepping out without a specialized suit would be deadly."),
 ('S08', 'An Orange Marble', 'ill', 'p03c', 'Temperatures on Titan hover near minus one hundred and eighty degrees Celsius, and ordinary water ice there is as hard as granite.'),
 ('S09', 'The Scale of Cold', 'ill', 'p04a', 'Try to picture the scale of that cold. A mountain on Titan is built of material that, on Earth, would melt in your hand.'),
 ('S10', 'The Scale of Cold', 'ill', 'p04b', 'It is a good reminder that words like rock and ice depend entirely on temperature, and that a different world can rewrite what everyday materials do.'),
 ('S11', 'Familiar and Alien', 'ill', 'p05a', 'In a strange way, Titan is one of the most Earth-like places in the solar system in its structure. A thick nitrogen atmosphere, weather, rivers, and seas. It is also one of the coldest places we could imagine visiting. It is familiar and alien at the same time.'),
 ('S12', 'Rivers of Methane', 'ill', 'p06a', 'How could such a brutally cold world ever harbor liquid lakes and flowing rivers? The secret lies in chemistry that feels like science fiction. While water on Titan is frozen solid, hydrocarbons like methane and ethane stay liquid at that temperature.'),
 ('S13', 'Rivers of Methane', 'ill', 'p06b', 'Clouds of methane gather in the hazy amber sky, condense, and pour down as alien rain. These showers feed rivers that carve winding valleys across the terrain, eventually spilling into immense polar seas. Could any living thing actually survive in such a bizarre, freezing environment?'),
 ('S14', 'A Polar Shore', 'ill', 'p07a', 'Imagine standing on a polar shore and watching a slow wave roll across a sea of liquid methane and ethane. It would not look like water, and it would not smell like anything you know, but the physics of waves and rain and rivers would be recognizable. Nature, it seems, likes to reuse its patterns wherever it can.'),
 ('S15', 'A Lake of Fuel', 'ill', 'p08a', "When we hear the word lake, we picture water. On Titan, a lake is made of something we would call a fuel. The rain falls, the rivers run, and the seas fill, all following the same pattern as Earth's water cycle, but with a different substance playing the role of water. This is a world with weather and geography, running on entirely different chemistry."),
 ('S16', 'Beneath the Haze', 'ill', 'p09a', 'For a long time, Titan remained shrouded in mystery because its golden smog blocked our view of the surface in visible light. What lay hidden beneath that veil? That changed when the Cassini spacecraft arrived at Saturn and released the Huygens probe toward the surface.'),
 ('S17', 'Beneath the Haze', 'ill', 'p09b', 'In January of 2005, Huygens drifted down beneath its parachute, and its cameras revealed coastlines, drainage channels, and a landscape strewn with rounded pebbles. Other Cassini instruments later mapped fields of sand dunes, rivers, and lakes. Scientists were stunned to see how much of it looked like Earth.'),
 ('S18', 'Not a Quiet Moon', 'ill', 'p10a', 'And there is a deeper lesson in this discovery. Astronomers had assumed that a moon so cold would be quiet. The surprise was that weather and erosion, two processes we think of as belonging to Earth, were shaping this distant place as well. It is a gentle warning against assuming that the unfamiliar must be simple.'),
 ('S19', 'A Hidden Surface', 'ill', 'p11a', 'Think about how frustrating that must have been for astronomers. For decades, a whole moon sat in plain view, and its surface was hidden behind a haze that no ordinary telescope could see through. Then, once a probe finally descended, the pictures showed something startlingly familiar. Coastlines.'),
 ('S20', 'A Hidden Surface', 'ill', 'p11b', 'Channels where liquid had flowed. The world turned out to be not a frozen relic, but a place where things are still happening.'),
 ('S21', 'Tholins', 'ill', 'p12a', "High in Titan's upper atmosphere, sunlight and energetic particles smash into nitrogen and methane molecules, splitting them apart. These fragments recombine into complex organic compounds, forming the orange haze. Scientists call these compounds tholins."),
 ('S22', 'Tholins', 'ill', 'p12b', "Slowly, over countless millennia, grains of this carbon-rich material drift down to carpet the moon's surface. Think of it as a cosmic kitchen where some of the basic ingredients of life's chemistry have been accumulating for a very long time."),
 ('S23', 'Sunlight as a Hammer', 'ill', 'p13a', 'The word tholins may sound technical, but the idea is simple. Sunlight and energetic particles act like a hammer on the molecules in the upper air, and the broken pieces reassemble into larger, more complicated carbon-based compounds. Those drift down and settle on the surface.'),
 ('S24', 'Sunlight as a Hammer', 'ill', 'p13b', 'That tells us the raw ingredients of chemistry, the kind of building blocks that life on Earth also depends on, are being made there naturally. But making ingredients is not the same as making life, and scientists are careful to say so.'),
 ('S25', 'Ingredients Are Not Life', 'ill', 'p14a', 'Think of a kitchen full of flour, eggs, and sugar. The ingredients are all there, but there is no guarantee anyone is baking a cake. Could these organic molecules ever assemble themselves into something that copies itself, without liquid water? No one knows.'),
 ('S26', 'Ingredients Are Not Life', 'ill', 'p14b', 'It is one of the great unsolved problems of astrobiology, and it is the reason scientists keep looking.'),
 ('S27', 'Life Without Water', 'ill', 'p15a', 'Here is where planetary science takes a fascinating turn. On Earth, life depends on liquid water to dissolve nutrients and carry out its chemical reactions. But some scientists have proposed that, under the right conditions, life on Titan might use liquid methane as its solvent instead.'),
 ('S28', 'Life Without Water', 'ill', 'p15b', 'One idea is that hypothetical organisms in those frigid lakes would breathe hydrogen instead of oxygen and consume acetylene for energy. They would be fundamentally alien, with cell membranes built without the water-loving molecules that define every creature on Earth.'),
 ('S29', 'Life Without Water', 'ill', 'p15c', 'Would we even recognize such strange life if it swam right beneath our robotic cameras?'),
 ('S30', 'A Different Membrane', 'ill', 'p16a', 'Consider what is being asked of these imagined creatures. The cell membrane is the thin wall that separates a living thing from its surroundings, and on Earth it is built from molecules that get along with water.'),
 ('S31', 'A Different Membrane', 'ill', 'p16b', 'In a lake of methane, a membrane would need a completely different design. That is why such life would not be a cold version of us. It would be something else entirely.'),
 ('S32', 'Only a Hypothesis', 'ill', 'p17a', 'It is worth being clear about what kind of statement this is. Life based on methane is a hypothesis. No one has found such an organism, and no one has shown that one is possible.'),
 ('S33', 'Only a Hypothesis', 'ill', 'p17b', 'It is a thought experiment that scientists take seriously because it forces us to ask what life really needs. If life can work without water, then our definition of a habitable world is far too narrow.'),
 ('S34', 'Only a Hypothesis', 'ill', 'p17c', 'If it cannot, then we learn something important about why water is so special.'),
 ('S35', 'The Hidden Ocean', 'ill', 'p18a', "Yet Titan has another secret waiting deep beneath its icy crust. Gravity measurements and the way the moon flexes under Saturn's pull strongly suggest that a global ocean of liquid water lies buried underground."),
 ('S36', 'The Hidden Ocean', 'ill', 'p18b', "While the surface is locked in a methane-drenched deep freeze, this hidden ocean may be kept from freezing by heat from the moon's interior and by the constant squeezing and stretching of tides."),
 ('S37', 'The Hidden Ocean', 'ill', 'p18c', 'In other words, Titan may offer two completely different potential habitats for life on a single world. An exotic hydrocarbon environment might exist on its surface, while familiar, water-based microbial life could, in principle, exist in the dark ocean below. Where else in the solar system can you find such a combination?'),
 ('S38', 'Warmed by Tides', 'ill', 'p19a', "Think about why an ocean can stay liquid in a place so cold. Saturn's gravity keeps squeezing and stretching the moon, and that flexing generates heat. It is a reminder that a world does not need a bright sun to be warm inside. Energy can come from the pull of a giant neighbor."),
 ('S39', 'Two Habitats', 'ill', 'p20a', 'Notice how this changes the way we think about the moon. A single world with two possible hiding places for life. On top, an exotic frozen landscape of liquid fuel. Beneath, a dark ocean of liquid water.'),
 ('S40', 'Two Habitats', 'ill', 'p20b', 'The evidence for that ocean comes from measuring how the moon flexes and how its gravity behaves, and the word scientists use is suggest. That is an honest word. It means the measurements point strongly toward an ocean, even though no one has dipped a probe into it.'),
 ('S41', 'Meet Dragonfly', 'ill', 'p21a', "To unravel these puzzles, NASA is building an extraordinary rotorcraft called Dragonfly, designed to fly across Titan's dunes and craters. Because Titan has low gravity and remarkably thick air, flying there takes far less effort than flying on Earth."),
 ('S42', 'Meet Dragonfly', 'ill', 'p21b', 'Can you imagine the first aerial footage sent back from an alien moon? The plan is for the drone to travel in flights across the landscape, covering roughly a hundred kilometers over its mission, analyzing the organic chemistry of different terrains and searching for chemical signs of life.'),
 ('S43', 'Meet Dragonfly', 'ill', 'p21c', 'It is expected to reach Titan in the mid twenty thirties. Each stop could bring humanity closer to answering whether life is an inevitable outcome of organic chemistry or an impossible miracle exclusive to our pale blue dot.'),
 ('S44', 'Flying on Titan', 'ill', 'p22a', 'Think about what flying on another world would mean. On Earth, a rotorcraft must fight gravity and thin air. On Titan, the thick air and low gravity do much of the work.'),
 ('S45', 'Flying on Titan', 'ill', 'p22b', 'That makes it a rare place where a flying robot can cover a lot of ground, visiting different kinds of terrain and sampling the chemistry of each. It turns a distant moon from a single snapshot into a landscape we can explore.'),
 ('S46', 'A Story of Patience', 'ill', 'p23a', 'It is also a story about patience. Missions to the outer solar system take years to build and years to travel, and the people who plan them often work for much of their careers before the first picture comes back.'),
 ('S47', 'A Story of Patience', 'ill', 'p23b', 'The reward is a view of a place that no human has ever seen, and a chance to test an idea that has lived only on paper.'),
 ('S48', 'One Sample at a Time', 'ill', 'p24a', 'Every sample could add a new piece to the puzzle. Is organic chemistry something that tends to turn into life whenever the conditions allow? Or is life a one-time miracle that happened here, and only here?'),
 ('S49', 'One Sample at a Time', 'ill', 'p24b', 'Titan will not answer that question tomorrow, but each careful measurement narrows the possibilities. That is how science moves: by shrinking the unknown one observation at a time.'),
 ('S50', 'Another Way to Live', 'ill', 'p25a', "The next time you pour a glass of water or watch raindrops trace patterns on your window, remember that our way of living might not be the only way. Out near the rings of Saturn, a golden moon reminds us that nature's imagination may far exceed our own."),
 ('S51', 'Another Way to Live', 'ill', 'p25b', 'Perhaps life does not require a warm, sunny cradle with liquid oceans like ours to take root. Or perhaps it does, and Titan will show us why. Either answer would teach us something profound about what it means to be alive.'),
]
